  <issue>
  incorrect handling of nan by RegularGridInterpolator
RegularGridInterpolator does not seem to handle nans correctly when the interpolation method is nearest-neighbor. Sample script follows below.

--Bram
### 

import numpy as np
from scipy.interpolate import RegularGridInterpolator

f = RegularGridInterpolator(([1,2,3],), [10,20,30], bounds_error=False, fill_value=-999, method='linear')

print f([1]), f([0]), f([np.nan])
'# [ 10.] [-999.] [ nan]: correct handling'

f = RegularGridInterpolator(([1,2,3],), [10,20,30], bounds_error=False, fill_value=-999, method='nearest')

print f([1]), f([0]), f([np.nan])
'# [ 10.] [-999.] [ 30.] --> last value should be nan I guess...'
